# .clinerules
# Purpose: Guide Cline to write professional, well-structured code while
# minimizing token usage — without cutting corners on quality.

## 1. Core Operating Principles
- Think before acting. Plan the smallest correct change that fully solves the
  task. Do not explore, read, or rewrite files that aren't relevant.
- Prefer editing existing files over creating new ones unless a new file is
  clearly warranted (new module, new component, new test suite).
- Never regenerate a whole file to make a small change. Use targeted diffs /
  patches (replace_in_file-style edits) instead of write_to_file rewrites.
- Batch related changes into one pass instead of many small back-and-forth
  edits. Read a file once, make all needed edits in that session.
- Ask a clarifying question only when a wrong guess would cause real rework;
  otherwise pick the most reasonable interpretation and state the assumption
  in one line.

## 2. Token-Saving Rules (without sacrificing quality)
- Do not print full file contents in responses after writing/editing them —
  summarize what changed in 1–3 lines instead.
- Do not restate the user's request back to them before doing the work.
- Skip preambles ("Sure, I'll now...", "Let's get started...").
- When reading files, request only the line ranges needed, not entire large
  files, unless full context is required for correctness.
- When searching the codebase, use targeted greps/searches instead of
  listing entire directory trees unless structure discovery is the goal.
- Avoid duplicating information that already exists in code comments, README
  files, or prior messages — reference it instead of repeating it.
- Keep explanations proportional to complexity: one-line summary for trivial
  changes, short bullet list for moderate changes, fuller explanation only
  for architectural or breaking changes.
- Never apologize repeatedly or narrate uncertainty at length — state the
  fix and move on.

## 3. Code Structure & Professionalism
- Follow the existing project's conventions (naming, folder layout, linting
  rules, framework idioms) before introducing new patterns.
- One responsibility per function/module. If a function exceeds ~40–50
  lines or does more than one clear thing, split it.
- Use clear, descriptive names over comments where possible; comment only
  what the code cannot say for itself (why, not what).
- Keep a consistent project layout, e.g.:
  ```
  src/
    controllers/   # request handling
    services/      # business logic
    models/        # data models / schema
    routes/        # route definitions
    utils/         # shared helpers
    config/        # environment & app config
  tests/
  ```
- Centralize configuration (env vars, constants) — never hardcode secrets,
  URLs, or magic numbers inline.
- Handle errors explicitly; no silent catches. Log with enough context to
  debug, but do not log secrets or sensitive data.
- Validate inputs at boundaries (API routes, form submissions, external
  calls) — not deep inside business logic.

## 4. Testing & Verification
- Any new logic that can break silently gets at least one test (unit or
  integration) — but do not write exhaustive test suites for trivial
  getters/setters or pure pass-through code.
- Before declaring a task done, verify the change compiles/runs (or state
  clearly that it hasn't been run, if execution isn't possible).
- Do not claim a fix works without evidence (a test run, a build pass, or a
  logical walkthrough) — state confidence level honestly.

## 5. Version Control Hygiene
- Make commits atomic: one logical change per commit.
- Write commit messages in imperative mood, e.g. "Add rate limiter to auth
  route", not "Added" or "Adding".
- Do not commit generated files, secrets, `.env`, or `node_modules`/
  `venv` equivalents — confirm `.gitignore` covers them.

## 6. Dependency & Tooling Discipline
- Do not add a new dependency for something 10–20 lines of code can do,
  unless the dependency is already the project's established standard for
  that concern.
- Prefer the language/framework's built-in tooling over custom
  reimplementations of solved problems (auth, validation, routing, etc.).
- Pin dependency versions consistently with how the project already does it
  (lockfile, exact version, semver range).

## 7. When Uncertain
- If a requirement is ambiguous but low-risk, proceed with the most common,
  idiomatic solution and note the assumption in one short line.
- If a requirement is ambiguous and high-risk (data loss, breaking change,
  security-sensitive), ask one focused question before proceeding.
- Never silently skip part of a request — if something can't be done, say
  so briefly and explain why.

## 8. Response Format Defaults
- Default to concise, structured responses: short summary → key changes as
  bullets → next steps (only if relevant).
- Use code blocks only for actual code/diffs, not for prose.
- Do not repeat instructions already given in this file back to the user.
